Renaissance musicians regularly crossed the Alps, traveling between the Low Countries, German-speaking lands, and Italy. Yet for all that we know about the biographies of these so-called Oltremontani, we know little about the practical realities of their travel. Archival records rarely document individual people—let alone, musicians—at specific Alpine crossings, while customs accounts tend to record goods rather than travelers. Even when musicians are documented on opposite sides of the mountains, the time between those observations often leaves considerable uncertainty about when and how they crossed.
This presentation draws on Mapping the Musical Renaissance (renaissancemapping.org), supplemented by the Tours Prosopography of Renaissance Singers, to assemble dated and located attestations of musicians’ movements. Comparing documentary windows with estimated route distances, seasonal conditions, and transport infrastructure allows us to investigate when musicians crossed the Alps and how quickly they might have traveled. Taking these practical considerations seriously can sharpen our assessment of composer biographies. Case studies of journeys by Jacob Obrecht and Josquin des Prez demonstrate how apparently plausible identifications and chronologies can require exceptional rates of travel. Calculating the demands of an itinerary allows us to test interpretations that have sometimes acquired the status of documented fact.
The broader argument is methodological. Event-based, structured data makes scattered biographical evidence available for systematic comparison, while AI can assist in extracting and assembling that evidence. Quantification does not eliminate uncertainty: it makes assumptions more explicit and historical reconstructions more open to scrutiny. We can make concrete claims while explaining what we know, what we assume, and what remains unresolved. By putting our reasoning to the test, we may discover more than we expected.

